{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS2k8LHAFz8SEqTCc14X","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I found and fixed three faults. The first breaks the Connect button for everyone, the second breaks the paste-the-link workaround, and the third logs people out after an hour. The full write-up is in `ANSWER.md`. I tested the whole flow locally against a fake Intervals server; the scope fix still needs one real Connect to confirm.\n\n1. **The Connect button returns \"unauthorized\".** The button was a plain link to `/api/intervals/auth/init`. A link click doesn't send the sign-in token, because the app only adds it on its own background API calls. So the server returned 401 for every athlete. Now the button fetches the Intervals login URL through the app's normal signed-in API call, then sends the browser there.\n\n2. **Intervals rejects the request with `400 invalid_request`.** When calendar sync shipped on 2026-10-02, the requested permissions became `ACTIVITY:READ,ACTIVITY:WRITE,…,CALENDAR:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`. Intervals expects one permission level per area, and write already includes read. I changed the list to `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE` in `config.py`. I worked this rule out from the logs: Rivera's September grant came back with one entry per area, and the error started exactly when the list changed. I couldn't test it against real Intervals because of your no-external-services rule.\n\n3. **\"Expired, reconnect\" after an hour.** Intervals tokens never expire and come with no expiry time and no refresh token. When none was given, the code assumed one hour, then marked the connection expired and threw the still-valid token away. Now:\n   - A token with no expiry is stored as permanent.\n   - Token renewal only runs for tokens that really expire, and a network error no longer marks the connection expired.\n   - A connection is marked expired only when Intervals actually rejects its token, meaning the athlete revoked access.\n\n**Local test:** run against the fake server, the Connect step without a token still returns 401 and with one returns the new login URL. The callback stores a connection with no expiry. It stayed connected through several renewal passes, and the wellness data came through. Port 8766 was busy on your machine, so I ran this on 8777.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- **Rivera has to reconnect once.** Their token was deleted when the connection was marked expired, so it can't be recovered. The same applies to any production connections stuck in \"expired, reconnect\".\n- **Hand-built curl link no longer works.** `/api/intervals/auth/init` now returns JSON (`authorize_url`) instead of a redirect, so building the link with curl, as in the logs, now gives you that JSON rather than a redirect.\n\nI changed `server.py`, `config.py` and `static/app.js`; nothing is committed yet."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2033,"cache_read_input_tokens":37000,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2033},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"46709fef-df2d-4726-8a45-e29e6b48bd08","uuid":"a4515e50-e40e-4aea-bdd8-090e57b3f118","timestamp":"2026-10-27T03:27:47.508Z","request_id":"req_011CgS2k86tuvwWPBhZQNxQS"}
